{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we start by letting the roots of the polynomial \\(x^2 - mx + n\\) be \\(p\\) and \\(q\\), where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are positive prime integers. By Vieta's formulas, we know that:\n\\[ m = p + q \\]\n\\[ n = pq \\]\nWe are given that \\(m < 20\\), so we need to find all possible pairs \\((p, q)\\) such that \\(p + q < 20\\).\n\nFirst, we list the prime numbers less than 20: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19.\n\nWe will consider each prime number for \\(p\\) and find the corresponding \\(q\\) such that \\(p + q < 20\\):\n\n1. If \\(p = 2\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17 (since \\(2 + 19 = 21\\) which is not less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\\)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6\\)\n   - \\(q = 5\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 5 = 10\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 7 = 14\\)\n   - \\(q = 11\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 11 = 22\\) (not valid since \\(2 + 11 = 13\\) but \\(11\\) is the only valid \\(q\\))\n   - \\(q = 13\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 13 = 26\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 17\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 17 = 34\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have \\(n = 4, 6, 10, 14\\).\n\n2. If \\(p = 3\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13 (since \\(3 + 17 = 20\\) which is not less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 2 = 6\\)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 3 = 9\\)\n   - \\(q = 5\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 5 = 15\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 7 = 21\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 11\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 11 = 33\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 13\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 13 = 39\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have \\(n = 6, 9, 15\\).\n\n","prefix_end":1437,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1191,553,20194,279,19703,315,279,47311,17767,87,61,17,481,14619,488,308,57758,387,17767,79,57758,323,17767,80,59,701,1380,17767,79,57758,323,17767,80,57758,525,6785,10250,25780,13,3216