,10048,2900,279,19703,315,279,47311,400,87,61,17,481,14619,488,308,3,525,6785,10250,25780,320,1921,14312,12460,568,16246,429,400,76,366,220,17,15,4779,1246,1657,3204,2750,315,400,77,3,525,1052,30,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSuppose the roots of the polynomial $x^2 - mx + n$ are positive prime integers (not necessarily distinct). Given that $m < 20,$ how many possible values of $n$ are there?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"5cf7b8ca305c7d7a025959f056cbc91266a163b7c576ce4ace732a5d90287f98"},"seconds":39.033528566360474,"seed":947831997608845905,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by letting the roots of the polynomial \\(x^2 - mx + n\\) be \\(p\\) and \\(q\\), where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are positive prime integers. By Vieta's formulas, we know that:\n\\[ m = p + q \\]\n\\[ n = pq \\]\nWe are given that \\(m < 20\\), so we need to find all possible pairs \\((p, q)\\) such that \\(p + q < 20\\).\n\nFirst, we list the prime numbers less than 20: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19.\n\nWe will consider each prime number for \\(p\\) and find the corresponding \\(q\\) such that \\(p + q < 20\\):\n\n1. If \\(p = 2\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17 (since \\(2 + 19 = 21\\) which is not less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\\)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6\\)\n   - \\(q = 5\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 5 = 10\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 7 = 14\\)\n   - \\(q = 11\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 11 = 22\\) (not valid since \\(2 + 11 = 13\\) but \\(11\\) is the only valid \\(q\\))\n   - \\(q = 13\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 13 = 26\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 17\\), \\(n = 2 \\cdot 17 = 34\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have \\(n = 4, 6, 10, 14\\).\n\n2. If \\(p = 3\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13 (since \\(3 + 17 = 20\\) which is not less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 2 = 6\\)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 3 = 9\\)\n   - \\(q = 5\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 5 = 15\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 7 = 21\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 11\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 11 = 33\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 13\\), \\(n = 3 \\cdot 13 = 39\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have \\(n = 6, 9, 15\\).\n\n3. If \\(p = 5\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3, 5, 7, 11 (since \\(5 + 13 = 18\\) which is less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 5 \\cdot 2 = 10\\)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 5 \\cdot 3 = 15\\)\n   - \\(q = 5\\), \\(n = 5 \\cdot 5 = 25\\)\n   - \\(q = 7\\), \\(n = 5 \\cdot 7 = 35\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 11\\), \\(n = 5 \\cdot 11 = 55\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have \\(n = 10, 15, 25\\).\n\n4. If \\(p = 7\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3, 5, 7, 11 (since \\(7 + 13 = 20\\) which is not less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 7 \\cdot 2 = 14\\)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 7 \\cdot 3 = 21\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 5\\), \\(n = 7 \\cdot 5 = 35\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 7\\), \\(n = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 11\\), \\(n = 7 \\cdot 11 = 77\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have \\(n = 14\\).\n\n5. If \\(p = 11\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3, 5 (since \\(11 + 11 = 22\\) which is not less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 11 \\cdot 2 = 22\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 11 \\cdot 3 = 33\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 5\\), \\(n = 11 \\cdot 5 = 55\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have no valid \\(n\\).\n\n6. If \\(p = 13\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2, 3 (since \\(13 + 5 = 18\\) which is less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 13 \\cdot 2 = 26\\) (not valid)\n   - \\(q = 3\\), \\(n = 13 \\cdot 3 = 39\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have no valid \\(n\\).\n\n7. If \\(p = 17\\), then \\(q\\) can be 2 (since \\(17 + 3 = 20\\) which is not less than 20).\n   - \\(q = 2\\), \\(n = 17 \\cdot 2 = 34\\) (not valid)\n   So, we have no valid \\(n\\).\n\n8. If \\(p = 19\\), then there is no \\(q\\) such that \\(19 + q < 20\\).\n   So, we have no valid \\(n\\).\n\nCombining all the valid \\(n\\) values, we have: 4, 6, 9, 10, 14, 15, 25. There are 7 distinct values.\n\nThus, the number of possible values of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{7}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:20"}
